==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|foreseen}}, {{m|enm|forseen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|foresēon}}; equivalent to {{pre|en|fore-|see}}. Similar formations in {{noncog|nl|voorzien}}, {{noncog|de|vorsehen}}, {{noncog|la|prōvideō}}, (whence {{m|en|provide}} and {{m|en|purvey}}),  {{noncog|grc|πρόοιδα}}, {{noncog|pl|przewidzieć}}, {{noncog|ru|провидеть}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/fɔɹˈsi/|/fɚˈsi/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/fɔːˈsiː/|/fəˈsiː/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-foresee.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyph|en|fore|see}}
* {{rhymes|en|iː|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|foresaw|~n}}

# To [[perceive]] (a situation or event) [[in advance]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Proverbs|22|3|passage=A prudent man '''foreſeeth''' the euill, and hideth himſelfe: but the ſimple paſſe on, and are puniſhed.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|II|i|page=8|passage={{w|Ariel (The Tempest)|''Ariel.''}} My Maſter through his Art '''foreſees''' the danger <br> That you (his friend) are in, and ſends me forth <br> (For elſe his proiect dies) to keepe them liuing.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1838|author=w:Charles Dickens|journal=w:The Lamplighter
|passage="I '''foresee''' in this," he says, "the breaking up of our profession."}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=Danny Hakim|authorlink=Danny Hakim|quotee=Kathleen B. Hogan|title=Two Misdemeanor Charges in Lake George Capsizing|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221126041035/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/02/06/nyregion/06boat.html|date=6 February 2007|passage="If you '''foresee''' the event and you go forward, then you are criminally negligent," she said, adding, "This was a boat that navigated on the waters of Lake George since 1979. In terms of criminal negligence, there clearly wasn't the foreseeability that there may be in civil negligence."}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[provide]].
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Vicissitude of Things|passage=Great shoals of people, which go on to populate, without '''foreseeing''' means of life.}}

